The given text is a section of an academic paper discussing fairness constraints on rankings, specifically focusing on exposure-based fairness in the context of parental care and ranking systems. The framework presented allows for the first time to compute utility-maximizing rankings under various fairness constraints using linear programming. It also provides a general framework that can express any fairness constraint by utilizing probabilistic rankings and linear programming. 

The paper introduces a new approach to addressing fairness in ranking problems, which is the allocation of exposure between groups. The authors propose a general framework for computing utility-maximizing rankings under various fairness constraints using linear programming. They demonstrate its expressiveness by applying it to three different notions of fairness: demographic parity, disparate treatment, and disparate impact.
